{%- comment -%}
  Bassface product page — the main section of the product template.

  Replaces Dawn's sections/main-product.liquid, which stays on disk for any
  template still pointing at it. The stock section is a block system: vendor,
  title, price and the rest are reorderable rows a merchant composes. This one
  is a FIXED layout, deliberately. The boards describe one arrangement at five
  widths, and a reorderable info column can produce arrangements they do not
  describe. Blocks return in stage 4, for the accordion rows and the offers,
  where the count genuinely varies.

  What is NOT reimplemented is variant switching. This section keeps Dawn's
  <product-info> element and its DOM contract — the same data-section, data-url
  and #price-{{ section.id }} target — so assets/product-info.js goes on
  re-fetching the section through the Section Rendering API on every option
  change and swapping the price in. Rewriting that means reimplementing history
  state, ?variant= deep links and sold-out resolution for no visual gain.

  Two contract details worth knowing before editing:

    - The element renders safely half-built. connectedCallback subscribes to
      option changes and then returns early from initQuantityHandlers when
      there is no .quantity__input, so the stages below can land one at a time.
    - updateMedia is guarded on `media-gallery ul` existing, and setActiveMedia
      is called with optional chaining. The gallery here is plain markup with
      no <media-gallery>, and that is a no-op rather than a crash.

  Still to come: the zoom and offers modals, and the =<834 restructure, where
  the gallery becomes a horizontally overflowing strip below a title/price wrap.
  Until the offers modal exists every offer line renders inline rather than
  collapsing to "+ more offers", so nothing here points at a modal that is not
  built yet.
{%- endcomment -%}

{{ 'bf-breadcrumb.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'bf-size-selector.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'bf-accordion.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'bf-size-chart.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'bf-reveal.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'bf-price-roll.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'bf-main-product.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
<script src="{{ 'product-info.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
<script src="{{ 'bf-product-form.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
<script src="{{ 'bf-quantity.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
<script src="{{ 'bf-sticky-column.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
<script src="{{ 'bf-delivery-estimate.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
<script src="{{ 'bf-reveal.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
<script src="{{ 'bf-price-roll.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
<script src="{{ 'bf-accordion.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>

{%- liquid
  assign variant = product.selected_or_first_available_variant

  comment
    The middle crumb. `collection` is set by Shopify only when the visitor
    arrived through /collections/x/products/y, so it is the truthful answer
    when present — it is the route they actually took.

    Falling back to product.type means guessing at a collection, and the guess
    has to be checked: collections['no-such-handle'] returns an EmptyDrop, not
    nil, so a bare truthiness test passes and links the crumb at a 404. The
    `== empty` comparison is what EmptyDrop answers to.

    With a type but no matching collection the label still renders, unlinked.
    Losing the category from the trail is worse than losing the link.
  endcomment

  if collection
    assign crumb_label = collection.title
    assign crumb_url = collection.url
  elsif product.type != blank
    assign crumb_label = product.type
    assign type_handle = product.type | handleize
    unless collections[type_handle] == empty
      assign crumb_url = collections[type_handle].url
    endunless
  endif

  comment
    Selling points. `custom.highlights` is a list metafield that does not exist
    on the store yet — it needs the same treatment the size chart got, a
    definition plus an assignment script, and until then this falls through.

    The fallback is the size chart's own fit_note, which is already populated
    for all 92 products and already says the thing the board's first pointer
    says: "Oversized fit — size up if you like it roomier." One real pointer is
    worth more than three placeholders.
  endcomment

  assign pointers = product.metafields.custom.highlights.value

  unless pointers
    assign chart = product.metafields.custom.size_chart.value
    if chart.fit_note != blank
      assign pointers = chart.fit_note.value | split: '~~~'
    endif
  endunless

  assign offers = section.settings.offers | newline_to_br | split: '<br />'
  assign payments = section.settings.payment_methods | split: ','
-%}

<div class="main-product">
  {%- render 'bf-breadcrumb',
    current: product.title,
    parent_label: crumb_label,
    parent_url: crumb_url,
    home_label: section.settings.breadcrumb_home,
    label: section.settings.breadcrumb_label
  -%}

  <product-info
    id="MainProduct-{{ section.id }}"
    class="main-product__grid"
    data-section="{{ section.id }}"
    data-url="{{ product.url }}"
    data-update-url="true"
  >
    {%- comment -%}
      Three stacked squares on the boards, but the count is whatever the product
      has — the gallery is a column, so a fourth image extends it rather than
      breaking a fixed composition.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="main-product__gallery">
      {%- for image in product.images -%}
        <bf-reveal class="reveal reveal--curtain main-product__media">
          <img
            class="main-product__image"
            src="{{ image | image_url: width: 1546 }}"
            srcset="
              {{ image | image_url: width: 549 }} 549w,
              {{ image | image_url: width: 773 }} 773w,
              {{ image | image_url: width: 1098 }} 1098w,
              {{ image | image_url: width: 1546 }} 1546w
            "
            sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 55vw, 100vw"
            width="{{ image.width }}"
            height="{{ image.height }}"
            alt="{{ image.alt | escape }}"
            {% comment %}
              The first gallery image is the LCP element on this template, so it
              is the one image that must not be deferred or de-prioritised.
            {% endcomment %}
            {% if forloop.first %}
              loading="eager"
              fetchpriority="high"
            {% else %}
              loading="lazy"
            {% endif %}
          >
        </bf-reveal>
      {%- else -%}
        {{ 'product-1' | placeholder_svg_tag: 'main-product__image' }}
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      A custom element rather than a div: the column is taller than the viewport
      on a normal laptop, and a plain sticky offset would hide its last rows for
      good. bf-sticky-column.js measures it and hands the offset back. See the
      note there — it is the second attempt, and the first two CSS-only ones are
      written up because they look right and are not.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <bf-sticky-column class="main-product__aside">
      {%- comment -%}
        Title and price are their own box because the boards MOVE them. Below
        the two-column breakpoint they sit above the gallery strip rather than
        at the top of the info column, and the only way to reorder them without
        a second copy in the markup is for them to be a grid item in their own
        right. `display: contents` on this wrapper is what promotes them to one
        — see bf-main-product.css.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="main-product__header">
        <bf-reveal class="reveal reveal--rise">
          <h1 class="main-product__title">{{ product.title }}</h1>
        </bf-reveal>

        {%- comment -%}
          The id is the contract: product-info.js copies innerHTML from the
          re-rendered section into `#price-{{ section.id }}` on every variant
          change, so everything price-related has to live INSIDE this element,
          including the compare-at and the tax note. Anything outside it keeps
          the first variant's values forever.

          role="status" so the change is announced — the price moving silently is
          the failure mode of an AJAX variant picker.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="main-product__price-row" id="price-{{ section.id }}" role="status">
          <bf-price-roll class="price-roll main-product__price" data-key="price">
            {{- variant.price | money -}}
          </bf-price-roll>

          {%- if variant.compare_at_price > variant.price -%}
            <s class="main-product__compare">
              <bf-price-roll class="price-roll price-roll--strike" data-key="compare">
                {{- variant.compare_at_price | money -}}
              </bf-price-roll>
            </s>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- if section.settings.tax_note != blank -%}
            <span class="main-product__tax-note">{{ section.settings.tax_note }}</span>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="main-product__info">
        {%- if pointers != blank -%}
          <ul class="main-product__pointers">
            {%- for pointer in pointers -%}
              <li class="main-product__pointer">{{ pointer }}</li>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </ul>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          Every variant's formatted price, so a size click can repaint the figure
          from data already on the page instead of waiting for the server.

          product-info re-fetches this whole section on every option change and
          swaps the price in from the response — correct, and several hundred
          milliseconds of network before the customer sees the number they just
          asked for. This map is what closes that gap; the server render still
          arrives and still wins, it just confirms what is already on screen.

          Keyed by variant.title, which Shopify builds by joining the option
          values with " / " — exactly what bf-price-roll.js reconstructs from the
          checked radios, so no variant matching is reimplemented here.

          `| json` rather than bare interpolation: it quotes and escapes, so a
          currency format containing a quote cannot break the document.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <script type="application/json" data-bf-price-map>
          {
            {%- for option_variant in product.variants -%}
              {{ option_variant.title | json }}: {
                "price": {{ option_variant.price | money | json }}
                {%- if option_variant.compare_at_price > option_variant.price -%}
                  , "compare": {{ option_variant.compare_at_price | money | json }}
                {%- endif -%}
              }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
            {%- endfor -%}
          }
        </script>

        {%- render 'bf-size-selector', product: product -%}

        <bf-product-form
          class="main-product__form"
          data-section="{{ section.id }}"
          {%- comment -%}
            The section that draws the bag counter. Passed from Liquid so
            bf-product-form.js holds no theme knowledge — see the note there.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          data-cart-section="bf-header"
        >
          <form
            id="product-form-{{ section.id }}"
            action="{{ routes.cart_add_url }}"
            method="post"
            enctype="multipart/form-data"
          >
            {%- comment -%}
              Updated in place by product-info's updateVariantInputs, which finds
              it by the form's id. Nothing in bf-product-form.js writes to it.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ variant.id }}">

            {%- comment -%}
              <bf-quantity>, not Dawn's <quantity-input> — see the note at the top
              of bf-quantity.js. The short version: Dawn's stepper is styled
              through the same three classes its script needs, and those classes
              paint it from Dawn's colour scheme rather than from --bf-ink, so it
              stayed #121212 in every Bassface theme.

              The label is on the button, not on the icon: bf-icon's `label`
              makes the SVG role="img", which would put a second accessible name
              inside the one the button already has.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <div class="main-product__quantity">
              <bf-quantity class="main-product__stepper">
                <button
                  class="main-product__stepper-button"
                  type="button"
                  data-step="down"
                  aria-label="Decrease quantity"
                >
                  {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'minus', size: 28 -%}
                </button>

                <input
                  class="main-product__stepper-input"
                  type="number"
                  name="quantity"
                  id="Quantity-{{ section.id }}"
                  value="1"
                  min="1"
                  step="1"
                  aria-label="Quantity"
                >

                <button
                  class="main-product__stepper-button"
                  type="button"
                  data-step="up"
                  aria-label="Increase quantity"
                >
                  {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'plus', size: 28 -%}
                </button>
              </bf-quantity>
            </div>

            {%- comment -%}
              data-cta is the swap target. product-info re-renders this whole
              section on every option change but only copies a fixed list of ids
              into the live page, and the button is not on it — bf-product-form.js
              reads the server's new button out of here instead of deciding
              availability for itself.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <div class="main-product__cta-row" data-cta>
              {%- if variant.available -%}
                {%- comment -%}
                  Three moving parts, from the reference: a second arrow parked
                  off the left edge slides in while the label and the trailing
                  arrow both shift right by their own arrow's width. The button
                  clips, so the incoming arrow appears from nowhere and the
                  outgoing one is not seen leaving.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                <button class="main-product__cta" type="submit" name="add">
                  <span class="main-product__cta-arrow main-product__cta-arrow--lead" aria-hidden="true">
                    {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'arrow-up-right', size: 26 -%}
                  </span>
                  <span class="main-product__cta-label">{{ section.settings.cta_label }}</span>
                  <span class="main-product__cta-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                    {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'arrow-up-right', size: 26 -%}
                  </span>
                </button>
              {%- else -%}
                {%- comment -%}
                  The reference strikes the label and drops the arrow rather than
                  greying anything — there is no grey in this palette. Disabled
                  rather than a bare <span> so the state reaches assistive tech
                  as unavailability instead of as decoration on some text.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                <button class="main-product__cta" type="submit" disabled>
                  <span class="main-product__cta-label main-product__cta-label--sold-out">
                    {{- section.settings.sold_out_label -}}
                  </span>
                </button>
              {%- endif -%}
            </div>

            <p class="main-product__error" data-cart-error role="alert" hidden></p>
          </form>
        </bf-product-form>

        <div class="main-product__delivery">
          <p class="main-product__delivery-line">
            {%- comment -%}
              The dates are computed in bf-delivery-estimate.js, not here. The
              model is two stacked business-day windows — processing, then
              region-dependent shipping — and Liquid has no weekday arithmetic
              without a counting loop. Ported from the live theme, where the same
              code sat in a custom_liquid block in templates/product.json.

              The element renders its fallback region synchronously, so the line
              is never blank and never shifts; the IP lookup only refines it.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <span>
              {{- section.settings.delivery_prefix }}
              <bf-delivery-estimate
                data-region="{{ section.settings.default_region }}"
                data-detect="{{ section.settings.detect_region }}"
                >&nbsp;</bf-delivery-estimate
              >.
            </span>

            {%- if section.settings.shipping_policy_page != blank -%}
              {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'arrow-right', size: 14 -%}
              <a class="main-product__delivery-link" href="{{ section.settings.shipping_policy_page.url }}">
                {{- section.settings.shipping_policy_page.title -}}
              </a>
            {%- endif -%}
          </p>

          {%- for offer in offers -%}
            {%- assign offer_text = offer | strip_html | strip -%}
            {%- if offer_text != blank -%}
              <p class="main-product__offer">{{ offer_text }}</p>
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>

        {%- if payments != blank -%}
          <ul class="main-product__payments">
            {%- for method in payments -%}
              <li class="main-product__payment">{{ method | strip }}</li>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </ul>
        {%- endif -%}

        <div class="main-product__accordion">
          {%- render 'bf-accordion',
            label: section.settings.description_label,
            content: product.description,
            id: 'description'
          -%}

          {%- capture size_chart -%}{%- render 'bf-size-chart', product: product -%}{%- endcapture -%}
          {%- render 'bf-accordion', label: section.settings.size_chart_label, content: size_chart, id: 'size-chart' -%}

          {%- render 'bf-accordion',
            label: section.settings.shipping_label,
            content: section.settings.shipping_content,
            id: 'shipping'
          -%}
        </div>
      </div>
    </bf-sticky-column>
  </product-info>

  {%- comment -%}
    Product structured data. Dawn emitted this from the bottom of its own
    main-product.liquid and nowhere else, so replacing that section silently
    took the theme's Product schema — price, availability, images — off every
    product page. It is one filter; it belongs here.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">
    {{ product | structured_data }}
  </script>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Product page",
  "tag": "section",
  "enabled_on": {
    "templates": ["product"]
  },
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Breadcrumb"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "breadcrumb_home",
      "label": "Home link",
      "default": "Home"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "breadcrumb_label",
      "label": "Landmark name",
      "info": "Read by screen readers to announce the navigation. Not shown.",
      "default": "Breadcrumb"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Price"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "tax_note",
      "label": "Tax note",
      "info": "Shown beside the price. Leave blank to hide.",
      "default": "Inclusive of all taxes"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Buy box"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "cta_label",
      "label": "Add to bag label",
      "default": "Add to Bag"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "sold_out_label",
      "label": "Sold out label",
      "default": "Sold out"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Delivery and offers"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "delivery_prefix",
      "label": "Delivery line",
      "info": "The date range is appended automatically.",
      "default": "Order today, get it by"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "default_region",
      "label": "Fallback region",
      "info": "Used before the location lookup answers, and whenever it fails or the location is not recognised. Metro is the fastest bracket, so an unknown visitor is quoted the most optimistic timeline.",
      "options": [
        { "value": "Metro", "label": "Metro" },
        { "value": "South", "label": "South" },
        { "value": "NorthEast_JK", "label": "North East and J&K" },
        { "value": "Islands", "label": "Islands" }
      ],
      "default": "Metro"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "detect_region",
      "label": "Detect region from IP",
      "info": "Sends the visitor's IP to ipapi.co on every product page. Off means every visitor sees the fallback region.",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "page",
      "id": "shipping_policy_page",
      "label": "Shipping policy page"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "offers",
      "label": "Offers",
      "info": "One offer per line. Every line is shown; the \"+ more offers\" modal is not built yet.",
      "default": "BASS350 — Buy 2, get ₹350 off."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "payment_methods",
      "label": "Payment methods",
      "info": "Comma separated.",
      "default": "Visa, Mastercard, RuPay, UPI, Cash on Delivery"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Accordion"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "description_label",
      "label": "Description row",
      "default": "Product Description"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "size_chart_label",
      "label": "Size chart row",
      "default": "Size Chart"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "shipping_label",
      "label": "Shipping row",
      "default": "Shipping & Returns"
    },
    {
      "type": "richtext",
      "id": "shipping_content",
      "label": "Shipping row content",
      "info": "Leave blank to hide the row.",
      "default": "<p>Dispatched within 24 hours. Free returns within 24 hours of delivery, on unworn items with tags attached.</p>"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
